      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND AND PURPOSE",
          "content": "Lateral elbow pain has several causes, which can make diagnosis difficult. The purpose of this case report is to describe the examination of and the intervention for a patient with chronic lateral elbow pain who had signs of nerve entrapment."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CASE DESCRIPTION",
          "content": "The patient was a 43-year-old woman who had right lateral elbow pain for about 4 months, which she attributed to extensive keyboard work on a computer. She had a reduction in joint passive range of motion during \"neural tension testing,\" an examination procedure to detect nerve entrapment. This sign, in combination with other findings, suggested that the patient had a mild entrapment of the deep radial nerve (radial tunnel syndrome). The patient was treated 14 times over a 10-week period with \"neural mobilization techniques,\" which are designed to free nerves for movement; ultrasound; strengthening exercises; and stretching."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "OUTCOMES",
          "content": "The patient had minimal symptoms at discharge, was pain-free, and had resumed all activities at a 4-month follow-up visit."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION",
          "content": "Neural tension testing may be a useful examination procedure and mobilization may be useful for intervention for patients who have lateral elbow pain."
        }
      ]},
